Step 10 — Connect Lambda → DynamoDB
PURPOSE:
Why?
Now Lambda is doing:
API/Event
   ↓
Lambda
   ↓
DynamoDB

Instead of simply returning a message, Lambda persists the device data.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HANDSON:
Now we'll give our Lambda permission to write to DynamoDB.
1. Go to IAM
IAM → Roles → InterviewLambdaRole → Add permissions → Attach policies
Search:
AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess

Attach it temporarily for this lab.
Why: Lambda currently only has permission to write CloudWatch logs. We need to give it permission to access DynamoDB.
In production, we would not use FullAccess; we'd create a least-privilege policy allowing only the required table actions.

2. Go back to Lambda
Lambda → InterviewLambda → Code
Replace the code with:
import { DynamoDBClient, PutItemCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb";

const client = new DynamoDBClient({});

export const handler = async (event) => {

    const item = {
        deviceId: { S: event.deviceId },
        temperature: { N: String(event.temperature) }
    };

    await client.send(new PutItemCommand({
        TableName: "InterviewDevices",
        Item: item
    }));

    console.log("Device data stored successfully");

    return {
        statusCode: 200,
        body: JSON.stringify({
            message: "Device data stored successfully"
        })
    };
};

